.TH BUFFER_SKIP_PRED 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_skip_pred \- Skip bytes in a buffer based on a predicate function

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_skip_pred (buffer* b, int (*pred)(int));

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_skip_pred\fR function skips bytes in a buffer based on a predicate function. It takes a buffer and a predicate function as arguments. The function skips bytes in the buffer until the predicate function returns 0, counting the number of bytes skipped.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.
.IB int (*pred)(int)
A pointer to the predicate function, which takes an integer and returns 0 when the byte should be skipped.

The function returns the number of bytes skipped.

The \fBbuffer_skip_pred\fR function is intended to be used to skip bytes in a buffer based on a condition, such as skipping whitespace characters.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_skip_pred\fR function to skip whitespace characters in a buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int skip_space(int c) {
    return isspace(c);
}

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    buffer_add(b, "Hello, World!", 13);

    int skipped = buffer_skip_pred(b, skip_space);

    printf("Skipped %d bytes\n", skipped);

    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef